ates and adult rats</article-title><source>Eur J Neurosci</source><year>2004</year><volume>20</volume><fpage>3027</fpage><lpage>3034</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15579157</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b20-ndt-5-015"><label>20.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lipska</surname><given-names>BK</given-names></name><name><surname>Jaskiw</surname><given-names>GE</given-names></name><name><surname>Weinberger</surname><given-names>DR</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Postpubertal emergence of hyperresponsiveness to stress and to amphetamine after neonatal excitotoxic hippocampal damage: a potential animal model of schizophrenia</article-title><source>Neuropsychopharmacology</source><year>1993</year><volume>9</volume><fpage>67</fpage><lpage>75</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8397725</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b21-ndt-5-015"><label>21.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rex</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Voigt</surname><given-names>JP</given-names></name><name><surname>Gustedt</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Beckett</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Fink</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Anxiolytic-like profile in Wistar, but not Sprague-Dawley rats in the social interaction test</article-title><source>Psychopharmacology (Berl)</source><year>2004</year><volume>177</volume><fpage>23</fpage><lpage>34</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15167980</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b22-ndt-5-015"><label>22.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zamudio</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Fregoso</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Miranda</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>De La Cruz</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Flores</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Strain differences of dopamine receptor levels and dopamine related behaviors in rats</article-title><source>Brain Res Bull</source><year>2005</year><volume>65</volume><fpage>339</fpage><lpage>347</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15811600</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b23-ndt-5-015"><label>23.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Walsh</surname><given-names>RN</given-names></name><name><surname>Cummins</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The open-field test: a critical review</article-title><source>Psychol Bull</source><year>1976</year><volume>83</volume><fpage>482</fpage><lpage>504</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17582919</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b24-ndt-5-015"><label>24.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Paxinos</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Watson</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><source>The rat brain in stereotaxic coordinates</source><publisher-loc>New York</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Academic Press</publisher-name><year>1999</year></citation></ref><ref id="b25-ndt-5-015"><label>25.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Post</surname><given-names>MR</given-names></name><name><surname>Kopanda</surname><given-names>RT</given-names></name><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Progressive behavioral changes during chronic lidocaine administration: relationship to kindling</article-title><source>Life Sci</source><year>1975</year><volume>17</volume><fpage>943</fpage><lpage>950</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1238889</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b26-ndt-5-015"><label>26.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Maaswinkel</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Gispen</surname><given-names>WH</given-names></name><name><surname>Spruijt</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Executive function of the hippocampus in social behavior in the rat</article-title><source>Behav Neurosci</source><year>1997</year><volume>111</volume><fpage>777</fpage><lpage>784</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9267654</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b27-ndt-5-015"><label>27.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Daenen</surname><given-names>EW</given-names></name><name><surname>Wolterink</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Gerrits</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name><name><surname>Van Ree</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The effects of neonatal lesions in the amygdala or ventral hippocampus on social behaviour later in life</article-title><source>Behav Brain Res</source><year>2002</year><volume>136</volume><fpage>571</fpage><lpage>582</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12429419</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b28-ndt-5-015"><label>28.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wan</surname><given-names>RQ</given-names></name><name><surname>Corbett</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Enhancement of postsynaptic sensitivity to dopaminergic agonists induced by neonatal hippocampal lesions</article-title><source>Neuropsychopharmacology</source><year>1997</year><volume>16</volume><fpage>259</fpage><lpage>268</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9094143</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b29-ndt-5-015"><label>29.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sams-Dodd</surname><given-names>F</give